course: reflection
lesson: 17
title: Old guilt: secrets that grow and how to let them go
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Some guilt is old: it happened months or years ago, nobody found out, and yet it keeps popping up at random, while brushing your teeth, at a birthday, just before sleep, like a stone in your shoe you can never quite shake out.
explain: Two years ago, in a temper, Chloe pushed her little brother off the trampoline and he chipped his tooth; she told Mum he fell, and he was too small to remember; now every time he smiles she sees the chip: "He's got that forever. Because of me. And he doesn't even know."
explain: A secret like that grows quietly because it has nowhere to go; the mind keeps bringing it back because it is unfinished business, and Chloe forgets that she was seven, and seven-year-olds in a temper do things they bitterly regret.
explain: When she finally told Mum, Mum said: "You were seven. Thank you for being brave now." Telling did not change the tooth, but within a few weeks the guilt shrank to a small, quiet thing, and she was extra kind to her brother.
explain: Lies work the same way: they snowball, because each one needs another to cover it; the earlier the truth comes out, the smaller the mess.
fact: Old guilt is guilt about something long ago that was never told; it often returns at random moments for months or years.
fact: A secret can grow heavier over time because it has nowhere to go; telling a trusted adult usually makes it much smaller within weeks.
fact: Young children in a temper sometimes do things they regret; what you did at seven is judged as something a seven-year-old did.
fact: Saying something unkind in a moment does not define a whole relationship, but it is good to say sorry while you can.
fact: Hiding bad news, like a poor school report, usually makes it worse; most parents would rather know early than find out at parents' evening.
fact: Parents' evening is a meeting between parents and teachers about how a child is doing at school.
fact: A win by cheating rarely feels like a real win; giving back what you did not earn often brings relief.
fact: Saying "it was an accident" when it was on purpose is a lie; owning up to hurting someone is braver than pretending.
fact: A lie often "snowballs": it gets bigger as more lies are needed to cover it; "telling porkies" is UK slang for lying.
fact: If something scary on a phone keeps giving you bad dreams, telling a grown-up is the right thing; the friend who showed it usually does not get into big trouble.
fact: Sneaking out at night is dangerous because nobody knows where you are if something goes wrong.
fact: Blaming someone who cannot speak up, like a two-year-old, is unfair; owning up and helping fix it ends the guilt.
fact: Breaking a friend's secret to look good yourself is called being two-faced; a proper sorry can slowly rebuild trust.
exercise: Two years on, Chloe still sees her brother's chipped tooth and feels guilty. Why does it keep coming back? | The secret has nowhere to go, so the mind keeps returning to it | Guilt only comes back when a grown-up reminds you about the thing you did | Her brother is secretly reminding her every single day | Old guilt always means the person is cruel deep down
exercise: What did Chloe forget when she kept blaming herself? | She was seven and in a temper, and young kids do things they regret | She was old enough to know the trampoline would break his tooth, so it's worse | Her brother had pushed her first, so it was really his fault | Teeth always grow back, so there was nothing to worry about
exercise: What changed after Chloe told Mum? | The tooth stayed chipped, but the guilt shrank within weeks | The tooth grew back in by itself, so the guilt went away at once too | Mum stayed angry with her for years afterwards | Her brother remembered and never forgave her
exercise: Ethan called his grandma boring, then she went into hospital. What does he worry about? | That it might be the last thing he ever said to her | That calling someone boring can make them ill enough to go to hospital | That the hospital will not let any kids visit | That his grandma will tell everyone what he said
exercise: What helped Ethan when his grandma got better? | Visiting her and saying sorry; she laughed and said she knew | Never mentioning it so she would not remember | Telling her someone else had said she was boring | Staying away from her for a while, so he would not upset her all over again
exercise: Ava hid her bad report. Why did every day get worse? | The secret kept growing and parents' evening was coming | Reports get worse marks the longer they are hidden | Her parents had already found it in her bag and were just saying nothing | Hiding things always makes worries smaller in the end
exercise: Sophie won a race medal by cutting across the field. Why does she want to throw it away? | It is not a real win | The medal is the wrong colour and quite ugly | Medals are heavy and annoying to carry around | She would rather have come second in the race
exercise: Kian pushed a boy on purpose and said it was an accident. What would be braver? | Telling the teacher the truth and saying sorry | Saying it was the other boy who tripped him | Keeping well away from the boy until he forgets what really happened | Pushing someone else to show it was normal
exercise: Mia told her friends her dad is a famous footballer. Now they want to meet him. What is happening to her lie? | It is snowballing: it needs more lies to cover it | It is shrinking, because friends forget quickly | It will turn true if she believes it hard enough | It is fine, because famous dads are often far too busy to meet kids
exercise: What happened when Mia told her friends the truth? | They laughed, and it was fine | They never spoke to her again | They told the whole school she was mad | They made her pay them back
exercise: Theo has bad dreams after his friend showed him something scary on a phone. Why should he tell his mum? | A grown-up can help with what scared him, and his friend won't be in big trouble | His mum will be so cross that she throws away every phone in the house for good | Bad dreams only stop once the friend has been punished | It is better to wait and see if the dreams get worse
exercise: Jacob snuck out at night and nothing happened. Why does he still feel guilty? | Nobody knew where he was if something had gone wrong | He did not have enough fun to make it worth it | His friends were cross that he came back early | Sneaking out is only ever wrong if something bad actually happens to you
exercise: What did Jacob get for being honest with his dad? | A bit more trust, like a later home time | A harder punishment than if he'd stayed quiet | Nothing at all, honesty never changes anything | Permission to sneak out whenever he likes
exercise: Lily drew on the wall and blamed her two-year-old brother. Why is that extra unfair? | He cannot talk well enough to say it wasn't him | Two-year-olds get into trouble all the time anyway, so one more won't matter | Mum would have punished him for something else | Babies do not mind being blamed for things
exercise: Alfie told his friend's secret about a bad mark to look good. What is the word for that? | Two-faced | Brave | Honest | Loyal
exercise: What usually helps old guilt let go? | Telling someone and making up for it where you can | Waiting longer and longer, because guilt always fades away on its own | Punishing yourself every night until it stops | Making sure nobody ever finds out what happened
test: Chloe's mate says, "you was seven, that's basically a baby, stop being so delulu about it." ("Delulu" means not seeing things straight.) What's the kind, true version? | She was young and in a temper; telling Mum can help it shrink | It doesn't matter at all and she should never think of it | She should feel bad forever because the tooth is still chipped | She should tell her brother he did it to himself
test: What would happen if Chloe kept the trampoline secret for ten more years? | It would probably keep popping up and stay heavy | It would definitely disappear on her sixteenth birthday | Her brother's tooth would slowly grow back by itself | Her mum would find out from the tooth on its own
test: Ethan's cousin in Texas says, "Your grandma's fixin' to come home, y'all can make up." ("Fixin' to" means about to.) What is the best plan? | Say sorry when she's home, while he can | Never mention it, she has surely forgotten | Wait until she is ill again to say sorry | Tell her the hospital food made him say it
test: Ava's thinking: "If I hide the report till the summer, it won't matter by then." What would really happen? | Parents' evening would bring it out, and the hiding would make it worse | The marks written on the report would slowly get better while it is hidden away | Teachers never mention reports, so nobody would know | The report would be thrown out by the school anyway
test: In a film, a kid cheats to win a gold medal and is happy ever after. What's off? | Many cheaters feel the win is fake and can't enjoy it | Nothing is off; winning by cheating makes a gold medal feel extra good | Medals only count if you win them by cheating | Real cheaters always get caught on the same day
test: Kian's friend from Liverpool says, "Just own up, lad, it'll be sound." ("Sound" here means okay, fine.) Is he right? | Mostly yes; owning up is braver and usually turns out okay | No; owning up always makes everything much worse | No; it was an accident, so there is nothing to own up to | Yes, but only if the boy's arm has fully healed
test: Mia's mate texts: "lol just tell em ur dad's on a secret tour in Brazil." What would that do? | Make the lie snowball even more | Make the first lie go away for good | Turn the lie into the truth by Friday | Help Mia feel calmer for good
test: Theo thinks, "if I tell, my mate gets sent to jail." What is more likely? | A grown-up will help both boys | His friend will be sent to prison for showing a video | Nothing will happen and the dreams will get worse | His mum will be cross that he ever had dreams
test: Jacob's gran, a proper old Yorkshire lass, says, "Eh, nobody knew where you were, love. Anything could've happened." What does she mean? | No one could help him if something went wrong | He should have taken a torch and then it would be fine | It was fine because nothing bad happened that night | Night-time is always safe in a small town
test: Alfie says: "I said sorry once, so we're square, innit." His friend still seems wary. Why? | Trust takes time to rebuild after being let down | His friend just doesn't like sorry at all | Sorry only counts if you say it ten times | His friend has forgotten all about it and just feels tired today
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises guilt pertaining to a long-concealed wrongdoing? | It commonly recurs at random intervals and diminishes upon disclosure | It invariably ceases upon the elapse of a period of twelve calendar months | It is experienced solely by persons of a cruel disposition | It is intensified by disclosure and relieved by concealment
exam: Identify the principal reason for which an untruth is said to "snowball". | Each falsehood necessitates further falsehoods to conceal it | Falsehoods are habitually uttered during winter months | Untruths diminish in magnitude with the passage of time | The original falsehood is invariably forgotten by its hearers
exam: It is given that a pupil has concealed an unsatisfactory report from his parents. Select the response consistent with the established facts. | Early disclosure is generally preferable, as concealment tends to aggravate matters | Concealment is advisable until the report is superseded the following year | Parents customarily prefer to remain uninformed of such reports | The marks recorded upon a concealed report are subject to improvement over time
exam: In respect of the assignment of blame to an infant incapable of speech, determine which statement is correct. | Such attribution is unfair, as the infant cannot contest it | Such attribution is permissible owing to the infant's age | Infants are deemed responsible for all domestic damage | Such attribution relieves the true wrongdoer of all guilt
